  "live_records": {
    "q-253550": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253550",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5-n}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-0} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-1} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-3} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
