    "q-253556": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253556",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the set $C=(-2,-1].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(C).$The supremum of a set $C$ is the {{field-1}} of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $C$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $C.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $C.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $C.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $C$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $C,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(C)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the maximum of $C.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $C$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(C)=-1\\in C.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
