    "q-253561": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253561",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the set $B=(-\\infty ,\\pi \\,].$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\pi $ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\pi $ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\pi ,\\infty )=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\pi .$",
