    "q-253910": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253910",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/42f6ea3d1c14b0ccb8774e284dc6812486ace7eda512e9c1b94df27e09445e6e.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{2}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{2}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-253910-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
