    "q-253513": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253513",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/351e7bb3db6811be2a690fbd3fc2dc99282c4ff676e9116717d85515a298cf80.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $3.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
