      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/0d745d274250c84f6701ff22d17d0c6506a9f03f4a1a461e09f609a0ea11d99b.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[1,\\infty )=1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
