      "source_example_id": "e-16538",
      "knowledge_point": "Supremum and Infimum of a Function",
      "kp_id": "e-16538"
    },
    "q-253899": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253899",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/567aa249f2ed84b537d20d4137a28e0df1cd3e4c942f4cf305bd864da32b8bac.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?$\\,{sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
